+# Keep sessions in signed cookies
+
+For whoever changes login, deploys or session storage. Sessions live in signed cookies after accumulated session
+rows filled the database disk during Saturday pickup.
+
+## Context
+
+In 2023 larder moved from one server to two behind a load balancer, and sessions stayed in each server's memory.
+Members were logged out whenever a request reached the other server, and every deploy logged out everyone; the pantry
+volunteers stopped deploying during opening hours. Sessions moved to Postgres so deploys preserved logins without
+adding a service for the volunteers to operate.
+
+On August 3, the database disk filled up: the sessions table held 2.1 million rows, including every login since
+2023, because expired sessions were never deleted. Larder was down from 09:40 to 12:50 during Saturday pickup.
+Volunteers used a paper list, and eleven members left without their pantry orders because volunteers could not find
+them. Deleting expired rows restored service. [Issue 63](../../notes/issue-63.md) records the incident and the
+discussion; the signed-cookie change shipped on August 9.
+
+Deploys must still preserve logins, and volunteers still need few services to operate. The incident added a need to
+remove the accumulating session data and its maintenance burden. The team accepted losing server-side revocation
+of an individual session before its short expiry.
+
+## Options
+
+- **Sticky sessions at the load balancer**: no code change; lost because a deploy still logs out everyone on the
+  restarted server.
+- **Redis as a session store**: fast and common; rejected in 2023 and again during this change because it adds a
+  service just for sessions that only Tom could operate.
+- **Postgres with nightly deletion of expired sessions**: the original shared-store choice survives deploys and
+  adds no service. A cleanup job would prevent the accumulation, but adds a job to watch and keeps a database read
+  on every request. Left after the disk-full incident.
+- **Signed cookies**: chosen; remove the session store, its per-request query and its cleanup work while surviving
+  deploys with a shared signing key. The cost is accepting the
+  [revocation and rotation limits](../features/sessions.md#known-limits).
+
+## Decision
+
+Keep the member and expiry in a signed cookie that every web server can verify with the same key, implemented in
+[larder/sessions.py](../../larder/sessions.py). This preserves logins across deploys without a session store or a
+cleanup job. The [architecture](../architecture.md#how-they-connect) shows how it connects to the web app.
+
+## Consequences
+
+The sessions table has been dropped; session checks no longer read Postgres, and expired sessions cannot accumulate
+there. Operating sessions now requires the [shared signing key](../features/sessions.md#storage-and-signing-key),
+with [limits on revocation and key rotation](../features/sessions.md#known-limits).
